-- erwartet: M103
-- 686 -- **A traversal counter from a 64-slot table, used to index a 4-slot one.**
--
-- The sibling of `685`, and the more ordinary bug of the two. `685` needs someone to write
-- arithmetic on the counter; this one needs nobody to write anything unusual at all. Two
-- tables, one loop, and the index is simply the wrong counter -- the shape a rename or a
-- copied loop body produces on a Tuesday.
--
-- **MEASURED against the binary before 2026-09-07:** `gabbro pruefe` gave `0 errors`, and
-- the emitter wrote `s->slots[i].b = 1;` over an `S_slot slots[4];` inside a loop running
-- `i` to 63. The traversal's own bound is real and it is the bound of `Q`, not of `S`; with
-- the counter untyped there was nothing for `M103` to compare against, so it compared
-- nothing and said nothing.
--
-- > **This is the case that shows the repair is about the TYPE and not about the loop.**
-- > A rule that only knew "a traversal counter is safe inside its own traversal" would pass
-- > this file -- the index IS a traversal counter, and it IS inside its traversal. What
-- > refuses it is that `index into Q` and `index into S` are different types with different
-- > bounds, which is exactly what a human writing the two declarations would have gotten.
--
-- `messung/KLEMPNEREI-2026-09-07.md` §5.

module gift::counter_from_one_table_indexes_another {

table Q count 64 {
    slot {
        a : u32 in 0 .. 10,
    }
}

table S count 4 {
    slot {
        b : u8,
    }
}

impl fn uebertragen(w : ptr<normal, r> Q, s : ptr<normal, rw> S)
    effects { reads w.slots, writes s.slots }
    costs   <= 900 ops
{
    traverse i over slots of w by unvisited
        touches reads w.slots, writes s.slots
    {
        -- `i` is an `index into Q` -- 0 ..< 64. `S` has four slots.
        s.slots[i].b = 1;
    }
}

}
